 \subsection{Axiomatic Models and Litmus Tests}\label{sec:bg-axiomatic}
-Prior work uses axiomatic models~\cite{alglave2010fences,herdingcats} to specify which executions a memory consistency model (MCM) allows. MCMs define the values that loads in a parallel program may return~\cite{lcms}. A \emph{candidate execution} of a program is an execution graph of its memory events connected by relations such as program order (po) and reads-from (rf), which relates a write to each read that sees its value. The model's axioms, typically the acyclicity or irreflexivity of combinations of such relations, form a \emph{consistency predicate} that decides which candidates are allowed~\cite{herdingcats}. 
-Such models formalize many hardware and language MCMs~\cite{owens2009x86tso,herdingcats,pulte2018simplifying,batty2011mathematizing,lahav2017repairing,simner2020ifetch,simner2022vm,simner2025exceptions}.
